# Runtime state a copy produces: the object store's local root (objectstore.fs),
# created at the path the configuration names when the first object is written,
# and the token signing key the program generates on its first run. Both land
# beside the Go module the program runs from and belong to a deployment, not to
# the source tree.
/server/var/

# Air's working directory: the binary it rebuilds and the build log. A local
# development artifact, never source. See server/.air.toml.
/server/tmp/

# The local development configuration the repository's .env selects through
# DEMO_CONFIG. It carries the social credentials once they are filled, so it
# stays out of the tree; server/local.yaml.example is the tracked template.
/server/local.yaml

# The frontend's build outputs, which no checkout commits: the installed
# dependency tree and the built bundle. Everything a build writes under
# server/dist is ignored, so building the frontend leaves the working tree
# clean. server/dist has to hold at least one file for go:embed to compile (the
# Go program embeds dist/), so server/dist/placeholder.txt is tracked -- the one
# file a build never touches, force-added despite this rule. See web/README.md.
/web/node_modules/
/server/dist/
# The end-to-end tier's own bundle, a build product like dist. It has the same
# need for one tracked file: an e2e-tagged build embeds server/dist-e2e, and
# go:embed refuses a directory with no files, so server/dist-e2e/placeholder.txt
# is committed -- force-added despite this rule and kept in place by the
# harness's clean step. The exception below re-admits that one file while
# everything a build writes stays ignored.
/server/dist-e2e/*
!/server/dist-e2e/placeholder.txt

# The compiled server binary: a `go build` (or `go run`'s cached build) run in
# server/ leaves it beside the module, named for the package. It is a build
# artifact, not source. The e2e configuration's manual command builds the same
# program under the `demo-e2e` name beside it.
/server/demo
/server/demo-e2e
